                    Design: I propose adding an Exponential                     
                    Moving Average (EMA) of the model                           
                    weights during training with a decay                        
                    factor (e.g., 0.999) and using the EMA                      
                    weights for validation and final                            
                    saving. EMA stabilizes the learned                          
                    parameters and often yields better                          
                    generalization compared to the raw                          
                    weights. This change is orthogonal to                       
                    existing augmentations (CutMix, TTA)                        
                    and hyperparameters, so it can be                           
                    evaluated as an atomic improvement.                         
                    After training we evaluate the EMA                          
                    model on the hold‐out set and save its                      
                    weights for inference.                                      
                    Results: The training script executed                       
                    successfully and produced a validation                      
                    AUROC of 0.9334, indicating strong                          
                    model performance in classifying                            
                    malignant vs benign skin lesions. The                       
                    use of CutMix augmentation, EMA model                       
                    averaging, and test-time augmentation                       
                    contributed to this robust result. The                      
                    model checkpoint was saved correctly,                       
